      "settings": {
        "color_scheme": "paper",
        "heading_tag": "h1",
        "heading_size": "h2",
        "eyebrow": "Care and washing",
        "heading": "What shortens the life of a membrane.",
        "body": "<p>These are washable and meant to be washed. Almost everything that ends a waterproof sock early is heat, softener or abrasion \u2014 not wear.</p>"
      }
    },
    "wash": {
      "type": "content-columns",
      "settings": {
        "color_scheme": "paper",
        "layout": "list",
        "numbered": true,
        "heading": "Washing instructions"
      },
      "blocks": {
        "i1": {
          "type": "item",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Wash cool",
            "body": "<p>A laminate fails from heat long before it fails from age. Cool wash, gentle cycle.</p>"
          }
        },
        "i2": {
          "type": "item",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Turn them inside out",
            "body": "<p>It puts the knit face, not the lining, against the drum.</p>"
          }
        },
        "i3": {
          "type": "item",
          "settings": {
            "title": "No fabric softener",
            "body": "<p>Softener coats the pores the membrane breathes through. Once they are clogged the sock still keeps water out, but it stops moving vapour.</p>"
          }
        },
        "i4": {
          "type": "item",
          "settings": {
            "title": "No bleach",
            "body": "<p>It attacks the laminate, not just the colour.</p>"
          }
        },
        "i5": {
          "type": "item",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Air dry",
            "body": "<p>Away from a radiator. A membrane does not need heat to dry, and heat is the thing most likely to end it.</p>"
          }
        },
        "i6": {
          "type": "item",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Never tumble dry or iron",
            "body": "<p>Both are heat, applied directly.</p>"
          }
        }
      },
      "block_order": [
        "i1",
        "i2",
        "i3",
        "i4",
        "i5",
        "i6"
      ]
    },
    "damage": {
      "type": "content-columns",
      "settings": {
        "color_scheme": "wash",
        "layout": "list",
        "numbered": false,
        "heading": "What damages the membrane"
      },
      "blocks": {
        "i1": {
          "type": "item",
          "settings": {
            "title": "They are not indestructible.",
            "body": "<p>A membrane is a membrane. Abrasion, toenails and the wrong wash cycle will eventually end one.</p>"
          }
        },
        "i2": {
          "type": "item",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Breathable does not mean dry inside.",
            "body": "<p>Work hard enough and you will sweat faster than any membrane can move vapour. Breathability slows that. It does not repeal it.</p>"
          }
        }
      },
      "block_order": [
        "i1",
        "i2"
      ]
    },
    "life": {
      "type": "centre-note",
      "settings": {
        "color_scheme": "paper",
        "heading_size": "h3",
        "eyebrow": "Expected lifespan",
        "heading": "We do not publish a figure for this.",
        "body": "<p>We have no wear-test data yet, and a number with nothing behind it is worth nothing. What we can tell you is the mechanism: a membrane ends through abrasion, heat and clogged pores. Wash it cool, keep softener away from it and keep your toenails short, and you are addressing all three.</p>",
        "footnote": "<p>When there is real wear-test data it will be published here, including the results that do not flatter us.</p>"
      }
    },
